From 515f56e6eee693289895f1caf09b56fadf0425a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 18:49:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Store the geolocation engine's own length/width, don't
 re-derive it

The CSV geolocation engine (src/geolocation/csv_engine.py) already computes
each detection's real-world length_m/width_m directly from its own slant-
range geometry (bbox_w_px/bbox_h_px * range_per_pixel_m), but that result
was being thrown away after insert -- GET /logs/{id}/detections and the
report writers silently recomputed a size from a reconstructed bbox via the
generic class_taxonomy.bbox_dimensions_m() helper instead, which uses a
different bbox-axis convention than the engine itself. For CSV-sourced
detections, the numbers the frontend showed were not what the geolocation
engine actually calculated.

- detections table (SQLite + PostGIS) gains length_m/width_m columns,
  populated at insert time for CSV-engine detections (still NULL for
  image/YOLO-pipeline detections, which have no equivalent physics-based
  sizing step).
- class_taxonomy.resolved_dimensions_m() is the new single source of truth:
  prefers a detection's own stored length_m/width_m when present, falls
  back to the existing bbox * pixels_to_meters estimate otherwise. Used by
  GET /logs/{id}/detections, report.json/report.csv, and now report.sql
  (which previously had no length_m/width_m columns at all).
- csv_engine.py's _to_detection_row() now carries geo["length_m"]/
  geo["width_m"] through into the stored row instead of discarding them.

New/extended tests lock in that a CSV-engine detection's stored dimensions
come back verbatim end-to-end (engine -> DB -> API -> report.sql), rather
than being silently overwritten by the bbox-based fallback.

36 passed, 2 warnings (backend/tests).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1cXxHLvxWsVApjc9w7bwH
